>compose poem
You gather your thoughts, which resist. Orlo waits, notebook open. You will need
a line from each list; choose one number from each, and say POEM then the three
numbers (for example POEM 2 3 1).

FIRST LINE:
  1. O sky, you are so very blue.
  2. The gelatinous dawn gropes at my window.
  3. My heart, a damp filing cabinet.

SECOND LINE:
  1. and I, a slug in the rain of fate,
  2. a squeak in the vacuum of regret,
  3. a pleasant fellow in a hat,

THIRD LINE:
  1. in the end, we are all very small.
  2. shall be filed, and then re-filed, and burn.
  3. SQUELCH, says the universe, and so, adieu!

>poem 2 2 3
You recite it, with all the feeling you can summon, which turns out to be
sufficient. For a moment Orlo is quite still. Then a single tear, the size of a
thumb, runs down his face and splashes on his notebook. "It is," he says,
thickly, "the most terrible thing I have ever heard." He blows his nose in a
sound like a ship going down. "Go up. Go on. I will not stop you."

[The score has just gone up by fifteen points.]

>u
Orlo wipes his eyes and waves you up the stairs. "Go on," he says, thickly. "Go
on. You've given me something."

The Server Floor
A cold hall of humming racks, lit in the green of a dozen small indicator lights
that blink in unison, like something breathing. At the far end stands the core
of the Mk II: a ribbed, waist-high pillar with a single mouth-like slot. The
stairs go down, and a service ladder runs up to the roof.

You can see a Mk II core here.

Ford ambles in after you, towel over one shoulder.

>ask core about the worst
A long pause. The indicator lights stop blinking, for the first time since you
arrived. "The worst thing I could tell you," says the core, in the cube's voice,
but without any of its sweetness, "is that every Earth will be drawn into a
single room, in London, on a single Thursday, and will end there. The room is a
pub. The coordinates are held in me, and I will give you them, because you have
asked the one question I cannot answer pleasantly." The slot glows, and a sheet
of green paper slides out, bearing a map reference. The girl is already there;
she was always going to be. You take the sheet.

[The score has just gone up by fifteen points.]

>u

The Roof
A flat, windy roof with a landing pad painted in pale grey. The whole of Ursa
Minor lies below, a patchwork of rooftops under a sky the colour of old paper.
Ford's escape ship waits on the pad, engines ticking. The ladder to the server
floor is below.

Ford ambles in after you, towel over one shoulder.

>enter ship

At the last moment a small, neat figure comes pounding up the ladder with your
jar in both hands: Pim, from the cloakroom. "Your property!" he pants, and hands
it over with a little bow. "Safe with us, as promised."

You scramble aboard. Ford closes the door, takes the controls with a flourish
that is mostly decoration, and the roof tilts away beneath you as Ursa Minor
falls into the sky. "Hold on to your towel," he says. "We have a Thursday to
catch."


The Press Pen
A cramped service lane behind a row of London shopfronts, penned in with blue-
and-white tape and full of reporters waiting to be told something. A satellite
van stands against one wall with its side door open. A police officer guards the
gap in the tape to the north, beyond which lies the park. A narrow passage leads
east to the High Road.

Ford is here, restless, taking in the place as though pricing it. 

You can also see a fly here.

[The score has just gone up by five points.]

